1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:04,700
Hi, I'm Edwin, student number 25092450.

2
00:00:05,330 --> 00:00:08,260
Did you know the Atlantic gets about 2 cm wider every year —

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,870
roughly as fast as your fingernails grow.

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:15,250
That's because at the mid-ocean ridge, the plates on either side are moving apart.

5
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:17,200
One side's speed is called the half-spreading rate,

6
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:19,530
and both sides together give the full-spreading rate.

7
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:21,830
But plates move so slowly —

8
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:24,170
so how do we know how fast they go?

9
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:25,550
This is the mid-ocean ridge.

10
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:27,750
The two paper strips are the seafloor on either side of it.

11
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:30,950
When I pull the strips outward, new paper comes out from here,

12
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:33,650
just like new oceanic crust forming at the ridge.

13
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:37,950
As the rock cools, it records the direction of Earth's magnetic field at that time.

14
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,100
The magnetic patterns on the two strips are perfectly symmetric.

15
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:47,150
Now suppose this stripe is 100 km from the ridge and 5 million years old.

16
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,950
Then the half-spreading rate is 100 km ÷ 5 million years,

17
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:51,550
which is 2 cm per year.

18
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:53,700
So the seafloor is like a magnetic tape,

19
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:56,980
recording every flip of Earth's magnetic field.

20
00:00:57,590 --> 00:01:00,830
Read the stripes, and we can measure the seafloor spreading rate.
